#
# The broker's own log and the per-job logs. This directory had no rotation rule at all
# and had reached 3.4GB across ~3000 job logs — a disk that fills is a broker that cannot
# write, log, or run anything, which is a worse outage than any it was built to survive.
#
# The durable health journal is deliberately NOT here: it lives under /var/lib, caps
# itself, and must outlive both rotation and the reboots it exists to explain.

/var/log/tt-device-broker/server.log {
    daily
    rotate 30
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
    copytruncate
}

# The per-job logs are deliberately NOT rotated here, by any stanza. They are write-once
# files that the broker reads back by globbing `*.log` for content — recent_jobs serves the
# ledger from them, and the job-id counter resumes numbering from the newest one. Rotation
# renames each to `<job>.log.1.gz` and leaves a 0-byte `<job>.log`, so a single nightly run
# blanks the whole ledger and drops the id floor while the evidence sits on disk under a
# name nothing reads. logrotate has no expire-only mode that avoids this: every stanza that
# matches these files renames or deletes the live file (`maxage` still needs a rotation to
# act, and `rotate 0` deletes today's log outright). Age-expiry is therefore a plain
# `find -mtime +30 -delete` in tt-device-reconcile.sh, which never touches a current log.
